{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RECLOSE-B586-A: the stale attestation was the SMALL problem. Its two children were ORPHANS.\n\nReopened to refresh a `stale-material` completion attestation. Verifying the acceptance criteria first\n— rather than reopening and discovering afterwards — turned up something bigger.\n\n### What was actually wrong\n\nThis ticket closed with **five unchecked acceptance criteria**, and two of them were **factually false**:\n\n    - [ ] both children exist, ARE CHILDREN OF THIS TICKET, and between them cover the whole scope\n    - [ ] both children are CLOSED before this ticket closes\n\n`list_tickets(parent=b586)` returned **zero children**. S1 `f2f3-9cb1-335b-4e31` and S2\n`6758-26b8-c9ea-4c5d` both had **`parent_id = ''`** — closed, certified, and hanging off nothing. They\nwere never children of this ticket, nor of the epic. A comment here has said \"BOTH CHILDREN CLOSED\"\nsince the split; the structure it describes did not exist in the store.\n\nThat also means the epic's descendant set never included them, so nothing ever checked their closures as\npart of this subtree. Both are in fact `certified` and `valid`, so no certification was wrongly granted —\nbut that was luck, not verification.\n\nIt closed with unchecked boxes because it predates the 433c AC-box close gate, which now blocks exactly\nthis. Several tickets in this epic share that vintage.\n\n### What was verified TRUE, before touching anything\n\n* **AC2 — recorded dependency link: TRUE.** `6758 depends_on f2f3` is present in S2's deps.\n* **AC3 — the four-round finding survives the split: TRUE, emphatically.** S2's plan carries it as\n  *\"SCOPE THE RETRY TO IDEMPOTENT READS. This is the load-bearing constraint\"*, naming\n  `_with_connection_retry` as the single choke point for all 15 transport call sites **including\n  `create_issue`**, and warning that retrying 429s there would reintroduce the duplicate-issue class\n  [rebar:21fc]. It also carries a per-member AC: *\"EVERY mutation call site still fails on the FIRST\n  429 — asserted per member over an explicit enumeration, not a sample.\"* Nothing was lost in the split.\n* **AC4 — the review record stays here: TRUE.** Six comments, including the ten-round history.\n\n### What I changed, and why this over the alternative\n\n**Re-parented f2f3 and 6758 under this ticket**, restoring the structure the split intended and that\nAC1/AC5 assert. The alternative was amending the criteria to say \"linked, not parented\" — i.e. editing\nthe requirement to match the miss. Re-parenting was preferable because the work genuinely was a\nparent/children split, the orphans lose their provenance otherwise, and both children are certified and\nvalid so the epic gains two descendants that need nothing.\n\n`child_closure_findings(b586)` now reports **0 blocking, 0 uncertified**.\n\nOne process note worth keeping: `rebar edit --parent` REFUSES a closed parent —\n*\"cannot re-parent to closed ticket … Reopen the parent first\"* — and I initially missed that because I\nechoed my own success string instead of reading the command's output. Two silent no-ops looked like two\nsuccesses until I checked `parent_id` directly."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785876787375039001,"uuid":"f02f279a-4038-47df-a9c5-2bdc1506e978"}